page cmd_usb {
  title = "vmlab usb"
  chapter_header "vmlab usb" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "4 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-19"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab usb` passes USB devices plugged into the host through to running
    VMs. It lists what the host has and which VM holds each device, attaches
    one to a VM, and gives it back. The device leaves the host while a VM
    holds it. See [Host USB devices](guide_usb) for the walkthrough.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab usb <COMMAND>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Subcommand" | "Meaning" |
      | "`list`" | "List the host's USB devices (hubs hidden) and the VM holding each." |
      | "`attach`" | "Hot-plug a host USB device into a running VM that has `usb = true`." |
      | "`detach`" | "Unplug a host USB device from a VM and give it back to the host." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  h2 "Selectors"

  p <<DOC
    `attach` and `detach` name a device with a selector. `vmlab usb list`
    shows the parts of each.
  DOC

  table {
    rows:
      | "Selector" | "Names" |
      | "`<vid>:<pid>`" | "The device with that vendor and product id, four hex digits each, e.g. `046d:c547`." |
      | "`<vid>:<pid>@<serial>`" | "The same, narrowed to one serial number." |
      | "`port:<bus>-<port>[.<port>...]`" | "The device in that socket, e.g. `port:1-2.1.4`. Always exactly one device." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    A selector that matches no device is refused. One that matches several,
    such as two identical flash drives without a serial, is refused with every
    candidate listed by its `port:` selector.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab usb list"

  code sh { source = "vmlab usb list [OPTIONS]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit a JSON array instead of a table." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    One row per device, in port order: `PORT`, `ID` (`vid:pid`), `SERIAL`,
    `SPEED` (`low`, `full`, `high`, `super`, `super+`), `ATTACHED TO`
    (`lab/vm`, or `-` while free) and `DEVICE` (manufacturer and product).
    Root hubs and hubs are hidden. The holders come from the supervisor, which
    asks every running lab. With no supervisor running there are no labs, so
    the host's own device list is printed with nothing held, and no supervisor
    is started.
  DOC

  code text {
    source = <<TXT
      PORT         ID         SERIAL  SPEED  ATTACHED TO  DEVICE
      1-2.1.4.1.4  046d:c547  -       full   usbtest/u1   Logitech USB Receiver
      1-3          0781:5581  BB22    super  -            SanDisk Ultra
    TXT
  }

  h2 "vmlab usb attach"

  code sh { source = "vmlab usb attach [OPTIONS] <VM> <SELECTOR>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]vm`." |
      | "`<SELECTOR>`" | "The device; see Selectors above." |
      | "`--force`" | "Attach a HID keyboard or mouse, which the host is probably using." |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit the attachment as JSON." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The VM must be running and must have been started with `usb = true` on
    its `vm` block or its profile ([Lab file: vm](ref_vm)). The attach is
    refused, in this order, when:
  DOC

  list {
    li "the machine is a container: `\"web\" is a container — USB passthrough is for VMs`;"
    li "the VM is not running, or was started without the controller: `\"plain\" has no USB controller to attach to — set usb = true on plain and restart it`;"
    li "the selector is malformed, matches nothing, or matches several devices;"
    li "another VM, in this lab or any other, already holds the device — the message names it as `lab/vm`;"
    li "the device has a HID boot keyboard or mouse interface and `--force` was not given — the host loses it while it is attached;"
    li "the vmlab process cannot open the device node `/dev/bus/usb/BBB/DDD` read-write. The message names the node and gives a `setfacl` command for now and a udev rule for good; see [Troubleshooting](apx_troubleshooting)."
  }

  p <<DOC
    On success it prints `attached 046d:c547 Logitech USB Receiver at port
    1-2.1.4.1.4 to usbtest/u1` and emits `usb.attached`. The attachment is
    pinned to that plug-in of the device: unplugging it from the host detaches
    it from the VM within about two seconds (`usb.detached`, reason
    `unplugged`), and plugging it back in does not reattach it.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab usb detach"

  code sh { source = "vmlab usb detach [OPTIONS] <VM> [SELECTOR]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]vm`." |
      | "`[SELECTOR]`" | "The device, as for `attach`. Required unless `--all` is given." |
      | "`--all`" | "Detach every device the VM holds." |
      | "`--json`" | "Emit what was detached as JSON." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The selector is matched against what the VM holds, not against the host,
    so a device that has already gone can still be named. The verb returns
    once QEMU reports the device deleted, and the device is back on the host.
    Stopping the VM releases every device it holds as well. Online snapshots
    are refused while a VM holds any device; see [Snapshots](guide_snapshots).
  DOC

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Find a device and hand it to a VM, then take it back:"
  code sh {
    source = <<SH
      vmlab usb list
      vmlab usb attach u1 0781:5581@BB22
      vmlab usb detach u1 0781:5581@BB22
    SH
  }

  p "Lend a VM the host's wireless mouse receiver, then release everything:"
  code sh {
    source = <<SH
      vmlab usb attach usbtest/u1 port:1-2.1.4.1.4 --force
      vmlab usb detach usbtest/u1 --all
    SH
  }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success. Exit 4 (`not_found`) means the lab declares
    no machine by that name. Every refusal above exits 1 (`failed`). A
    qualified reference to a lab that is not running exits 1. A usage error,
    such as `detach` with neither a selector nor `--all`, exits 2.
  DOC
}
